Developing Coalesce

Coalesce has two development loops and only one of them is fast everywhere.
Pipelines change at apply speed — edit the Zsh, re-run, seconds — and that
loop works the same on any cluster. The substrate is the other loop: the
receiver's Go, the executor's Zsh, the server. This file is the doorway to the
substrate's own fast loop — a Coalesce deployment for OrbStack's single-node
Kubernetes, on the Mac where the substrate is actually edited.

The local environment is the same programs as production with different
environment. There is no dev mode anywhere; the difference lives entirely in
manifests/local/, which is what keeps the local world honest about the real
one.

---

Prerequisites

OrbStack provides Docker and Kubernetes in one install. The Kubernetes
cluster must be running:

    orb start k8s

Verify:

    kubectl --context orbstack cluster-info

---

kubectl Context — read this one

Your Mac's kubeconfig almost certainly defaults to a real cluster. On the
machines this file was written for, the current context is production. Every
command below carries --context orbstack, and the flag is not decoration: a
bare `kubectl delete namespace coalesce` is the difference between resetting
a sandbox and deleting production's namespace, one omitted word apart.

To see where a bare kubectl currently points:

    kubectl config current-context

The executor calls kubectl without a context flag, so Mac-side pipeline runs
need a guarded environment. Generate a kubeconfig that can only see OrbStack
and export it for the run (shown below under Running a Pipeline):

    kubectl --kubeconfig "$HOME/.kube/config" config view --minify --flatten \
        --context orbstack > /tmp/orbstack.kubeconfig.new
    mv /tmp/orbstack.kubeconfig.new /tmp/orbstack.kubeconfig

The source is named outright and the write goes to a scratch name first, so
the command works no matter what KUBECONFIG already says — generating the
guard straight into a path that KUBECONFIG names truncates the file before
kubectl reads it, and every kubectl after that dials localhost and dies.

---

The Cluster Is Shared

The standing OrbStack cluster carries other tenants (penpot and friends, in
default). The local environment lives strictly in its own coalesce namespace.
Never `orb delete k8s` — that costs a colleague's running furniture. The reset
that is yours to run is the namespace, described at the bottom of this file.

---

Deploying

Build the images — server, receiver, source operator, and the dispatch image
that receiver-created pipelines run. OrbStack makes locally built images visible
to its cluster: no registry, no push, no pull secret.

    docker build -t coalesce-web .
    docker build -f Dockerfile.receiver -t coalesce-receiver .
    docker build -f Dockerfile.operator -t coalesce-operator .
    docker build -f Dockerfile.dispatch -t coalesce-dispatch .

Install the CRD, then apply the local manifests — namespace, PostgreSQL,
server, receiver, operator — and mint the webhook secret from a throwaway
value that matters to nobody:

    kubectl --context orbstack apply -f manifests/crd.yaml
    kubectl --context orbstack wait --for=condition=Established \
        crd/coalesces.coalesce.flatheadmill.com --timeout=60s
    kubectl --context orbstack apply -f manifests/local/
    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce create secret generic coalesce-webhook \
        --from-literal=secret=local-webhook-secret \
        --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl --context orbstack apply -f -

This creates the coalesce namespace; a PostgreSQL Deployment with its data on
a PVC; the server with its ServiceAccount, namespaced RBAC for pod log
access, a PVC for log storage, and a Service named coalesce; and the receiver
with its own ServiceAccount and namespaced RBAC (watch ConfigMaps, create
Jobs). The operator has its own ServiceAccount and namespaced RBAC to read
Coalesce resources, reconcile ConfigMaps, and record Events; it cannot create
Jobs. The server and PostgreSQL carry strategy Recreate on purpose — on one
node, ReadWriteOnce guards nothing, and a rolling update would run two
servers (stale-looking UI at exactly the wrong moment) or two postmasters (a
crash-looping database). The receiver pod waits in
CreateContainerConfigError until the secret exists, then starts — the same
fatal-and-retry arrangement as the server waiting on PostgreSQL.

The server pod may restart once or twice while PostgreSQL comes up: it exits
fatally when the database is unreachable, and Kubernetes retries until the
readiness order sorts itself out. That is the intended arrangement, not a bug.

Verify from the Mac — OrbStack resolves cluster DNS directly, so this needs
no port-forward:

    curl http://coalesce.coalesce.svc.cluster.local/health

The same address works from curl, the browser, and any process on the Mac.
Two is served at the root — the UI rides embedded in the server image,
built from studio/two by the Dockerfile's node stage, so deploying the server
deploys the UI:

    http://coalesce.coalesce.svc.cluster.local/coalesce/runs

The old prototype remains at /run.html until its role is fully retired. For
UI development the loop uses the same studio/two source with Vite on the
Mac, proxying /api, /events, and /tail to the cluster. With Node 22.12 or newer,
run from the repository root:

    npm ci --prefix studio
    npm run dev:two --prefix studio

Open http://localhost:5172/coalesce/runs. Set COALESCE_DEV_UPSTREAM to use a
different backend. The other exhibits and the separate studio container
remain available; studio/README.md describes them.

A bare `go build ./cmd/web` always compiles — a tracked placeholder satisfies
the embed on a clean clone — but a binary built that way serves a plain-text
notice at the root instead of the app. To embed Two in a bare build after
installing the studio dependencies, build it and copy its output into the
embed directory, removing previous assets while keeping the placeholder:

    npm run build:two --prefix studio
    find cmd/web/dist -mindepth 1 ! -name .gitkeep -delete
    cp -R studio/dist/two/. cmd/web/dist/
    go build ./cmd/web

The Docker build runs both stages itself and is the path that ships.

---

Testing the Manifest Renderer

With zshctl, gojq, bsdtar, and gzip on PATH, run:

    zsh test/make.test.zsh

This checks reproducibility across checkout timestamps, preserved source and
executable permissions, content changes, archive failures, and rejection of
live cluster-resource inputs. It runs locally without Kubernetes access.

---

The Source Operator

The operator accepts readable Coalesce resources and produces the same
ConfigMap that make produces from a directory. These are different source
forms even though they share apiVersion and kind: make still reads the
descriptor's outer metadata and archives the directory; the operator reads
spec.configMapMetadata and spec.files. make rejects a descriptor containing
either of those fields, including null or empty values, before emitting any
ConfigMap in the batch. CRD admission requires spec.files and therefore
rejects a directory descriptor. The receiver and the pods continue to consume
ConfigMaps.

The source's outer metadata belongs to its deployment tool. Only
spec.configMapMetadata.labels and annotations go to the child. Its name and
namespace match the source. Existing direct ConfigMaps are never adopted,
and an old source UID is not interchangeable with a new resource of the
same name. No migration is hidden in reconciliation.

Run the Go tests and the two-producer proof without a cluster:

    go test ./...
    go test -race ./cmd/operator
    go test -tags=integration ./cmd/operator
    zsh test/make.test.zsh

The integration test invokes make and bsdtar and compares extracted hello
and library trees, file modes, child metadata, and templates. It needs the
same local tools as the make test. The fixtures explicitly include their
old coalesce.yaml as a file so the extracted trees are equal; the operator
never invents that file. It writes regular USTAR files only, in sorted order,
with 0644 or 0755 modes. Paths that USTAR cannot encode, duplicate paths,
unclean paths, and file/ancestor collisions are rejected. Body bytes,
including the last newline, are preserved.

After deploying locally, use test/hello.coalesce.yaml and
test/library.coalesce.yaml for the live proof. If you previously ran the
direct-ConfigMap example below, first inspect its hello/library ConfigMaps.
For those known test fixtures, make the handoff explicitly:

    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce get configmap hello library
    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce delete configmap hello library

Keep a separate direct library to prove coexistence, then apply the readable
sources:

    ./bin/coalesce make test/library | \
        gojq --yaml-input --yaml-output '.metadata.name = "direct-library"' | \
        kubectl --context orbstack apply -f -
    kubectl --context orbstack apply -f test/library.coalesce.yaml -f test/hello.coalesce.yaml
    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce get coalesce,configmap hello \
        -o 'custom-columns=KIND:.kind,NAME:.metadata.name,GENERATION:.metadata.generation,RENDERED:.metadata.annotations.coalesce\.flatheadmill\.com/rendered-generation,OWNER:.metadata.ownerReferences[0].uid'

Wait until the child exists and its rendered generation matches the source.
The reserved coalesce.flatheadmill.com/rendered-generation annotation and the
owner-reference UID identify the source that produced the artifact. An
authored value for that annotation is rejected. This is evidence of rendering,
not receiver acceptance or a successful run.

Inspect the actual archive and the child owner reference:

    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce get configmap hello \
        -o jsonpath='{.binaryData.ball\.tar\.gz}' | base64 -d | bsdtar -tvf -
    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce get configmap hello -o yaml

Two different failures expose the two different owners. First give the
operator a tree it cannot extract:

    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce patch coalesce hello --type=merge \
        -p '{"spec":{"files":[{"path":"../escape","body":"bad"}]}}'
    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce describe coalesce hello

The ConfigMap's bytes and rendered generation must remain unchanged. The
operator emits an InvalidSource Warning on the Coalesce resource and logs
that the existing artifact was left in place. Restore the files, then give
the receiver a template with an unknown field:

    kubectl --context orbstack apply -f test/hello.coalesce.yaml
    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce patch coalesce hello --type=merge \
        -p '{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"unknownPodField":true}}}}'
    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce get configmap hello \
        -o jsonpath='{.data.template\.yaml}'
    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce logs deployment/coalesce-receiver --tail=20

The unknown field must survive CRD admission and rendering, and the rendered
generation must advance. The receiver's existing strict decode rejects it
and removes this claim from its cache. Its log explains the refusal. There
is no receiver Event or acceptance marker in this level. Send the synthetic
delivery from the Receiver section: after that refusal it returns no run.
Remove the bad field explicitly (it was added outside the apply manifest):

    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce patch coalesce hello --type=json \
        -p '[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/template/spec/unknownPodField"}]'

After the artifact catches up and the receiver logs Pipeline cached, send
another synthetic delivery and observe the Job complete with hello, world.
The operator proves that the artifact matches its source. The delivery and
Job provide the evidence about dispatch and execution. An empty template
also passes through; Kubernetes can still reject the resulting Job.

Exercise CRD defaults by removing executable from a library file and reading
it back; the API should default it to false:

    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce patch coalesce library --type=json \
        -p '[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/files/0/executable"}]'
    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce get coalesce library -o yaml

Delete the hello resource and observe garbage collection, while the direct
library remains:

    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce delete coalesce hello
    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce wait --for=delete configmap/hello --timeout=60s
    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce get configmap direct-library

Finally, prove the ownership handoff itself. Start after the hello resource
and its child have been deleted above. Use the OrbStack-only kubeconfig from
Running a Pipeline. First make kapp own a directly rendered hello ConfigMap:

    ./bin/coalesce make test/hello > /tmp/coalesce-operator-proof-direct.yaml
    kapp --kubeconfig /tmp/orbstack.kubeconfig deploy -n coalesce \
        -a coalesce-operator-proof -f /tmp/coalesce-operator-proof-direct.yaml
    direct_uid=$(kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce get configmap hello -o jsonpath='{.metadata.uid}')
    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce get configmap hello \
        -o jsonpath='{.metadata.labels.kapp\.k14s\.io/app}'

Confirm the kapp app label is present. In a second terminal, start a watch
and wait for the initial ADDED event with that direct ConfigMap's UID before
continuing. Keep this watch running through the handoff and its test delivery:

    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce get configmap hello \
        --watch --output-watch-events -o json > /tmp/coalesce-operator-proof-watch.json

Replace the app's ConfigMap input with the same-named Coalesce resource in
one deploy. Do not manually delete the ConfigMap between these two kapp
invocations; kapp must perform its own removal while the operator converges:

    kapp --kubeconfig /tmp/orbstack.kubeconfig deploy -n coalesce \
        -a coalesce-operator-proof -f test/hello.coalesce.yaml

The diff should remove the direct ConfigMap and add the Coalesce resource.
The source may initially report an ownership collision. After kapp removes
the old object, the operator must create a new child during this deploy.
Wait for matching source/rendered generations and inspect the new owner:

    source_uid=$(kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce get coalesce hello -o jsonpath='{.metadata.uid}')
    replacement_uid=$(kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce get configmap hello -o jsonpath='{.metadata.uid}')
    [[ $direct_uid != $replacement_uid ]]
    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce get configmap hello -o json | \
        gojq -e --arg owner "$source_uid" \
        'any(.metadata.ownerReferences[]; .controller == true and .uid == $owner) and (.metadata.labels | has("kapp.k14s.io/app") | not)'

Redeliver the synthetic payload and observe the hello Job complete. A delivery
in the interval without a receiver claim may need this redelivery. Stop the
watch with Ctrl-C, then check the whole trace, not just the final ConfigMap:

    gojq -se --arg old "$direct_uid" --arg replacement "$replacement_uid" \
        '([.[] | select(.type == "DELETED") | .object.metadata.uid] == [$old]) and any(.[]; .type == "ADDED" and .object.metadata.uid == $replacement)' \
        /tmp/coalesce-operator-proof-watch.json

Only the original UID may have a DELETED event. A deletion of an
operator-created replacement fails the handoff proof even if the operator
recreates it and the final state looks healthy. Finally, apply the unchanged
resource once more through the same kapp app; that diff must neither claim
nor propose deletion of the generated child. This last apply proves steady
state; it cannot substitute for the one-deploy handoff.

Fake clients cannot prove CRD pruning/defaulting, owner-reference admission,
garbage collection, or this kapp ownership behavior; those remain live
checks even when every local test passes.

For a portable installation, manifests/operator.yaml has the same RBAC and
binary with a registry image name. Create its coalesce namespace, install
manifests/crd.yaml, and publish or substitute that image before applying the
operator manifest. manifests/example-pipeline.yaml is a standalone example.
This does not migrate any Acres resource or publish an image by itself.

---

The Substrate Loop

After editing server Go code:

    docker build -t coalesce-web .
    kubectl --context orbstack rollout restart deployment/coalesce-web -n coalesce

Recreate replaces the pod with the new image (imagePullPolicy Never, same
tag). Check it came up:

    kubectl --context orbstack logs -n coalesce deployment/coalesce-web --tail=10

Logs stored before the rebuild are still retrievable after it — they live on
the coalesce-logs PVC, not in the pod — so the database's container rows and
the log store keep agreeing across rebuilds.

---

Running a Pipeline

The executor runs on the Mac and creates Jobs in the cluster. Three defaults
in the code all whisper "default" — the executor's namespace, its server URL,
and the server's watch namespace. The manifests override the server's; the
invocation must override the executor's two, and guard kubectl:

    kubectl --kubeconfig "$HOME/.kube/config" config view --minify --flatten \
        --context orbstack > /tmp/orbstack.kubeconfig.new
    mv /tmp/orbstack.kubeconfig.new /tmp/orbstack.kubeconfig
    export KUBECONFIG=/tmp/orbstack.kubeconfig
    export COALESCE_URL=http://coalesce.coalesce.svc.cluster.local
    ./bin/coalesce run -s hello-1 -N coalesce test/one.coalesce.zsh

The -s flag is the slug — the run's identity. Re-running with the same slug
resumes: completed jobs are skipped. A fresh slug is a fresh run.

Verify the run recorded, and that nothing leaked into default:

    curl http://coalesce.coalesce.svc.cluster.local/api/coalesce/runs
    kubectl --context orbstack get jobs -n default

The first command shows the run row; the second must stay empty. If the row
turned up under /api/default/runs instead, or Jobs appeared in default, an
override was missed.

The run table's first honest test data is three runs side by side:

    ./bin/coalesce run -s hello-1 -N coalesce test/one.coalesce.zsh
    ./bin/coalesce run -s cracked-widget-1 -N coalesce test/cracked-widget.coalesce.zsh
    ./bin/coalesce run -s widget-press-1 -N coalesce test/widget-press.coalesce.zsh &

The first completes, the second fails on purpose, the third stamps a widget a
second for three minutes — completed, failed, and running in one listing.

Retrieve a harvested log (the server's pod watcher collects logs from
terminated pods into the log store):

    curl http://coalesce.coalesce.svc.cluster.local/api/coalesce/logs/hello-1/coalesce.printer/printer

---

The Receiver and Synthetic Deliveries

GitHub cannot reach a laptop and should not: a signed payload from curl is
byte-for-byte what a webhook is, and the local webhook secret is whatever the
mint above said it is.

A pipeline is a directory whose coalesce.yaml is a typed descriptor —
apiVersion coalesce.flatheadmill.com/v1alpha1, kind Coalesce — whose metadata
becomes the ConfigMap's verbatim (the claim label, the repository and events
annotations) and whose spec.template is the pod the receiver wraps in a Job.
test/hello is the example: it mounts its own ConfigMap and the library
ConfigMap, unpacks both with an authored layout program, and hands over to
its run file. Render and apply both:

    ./bin/coalesce make test/hello test/library | kubectl --context orbstack apply -f -

The receiver logs "Pipeline cached" when it accepts a claim. Then deliver.
The payload needs the fields the envelope and the exports read — number,
pull_request.merged, pull_request.merge_commit_sha, sender.login — and the
repository must belong to the organization the receiver was told
(amalgamated-widgets locally, the fictional proprietor of all example
content):

    cat > /tmp/delivery.json <<'PAYLOAD'
    {
      "action": "opened",
      "number": 42,
      "pull_request": {
        "merged": false,
        "merge_commit_sha": "0000000000000000000000000000000000000000"
      },
      "sender": {
        "login": "widget-crank"
      },
      "repository": {
        "full_name": "amalgamated-widgets/hello-widget"
      }
    }
    PAYLOAD
    signature="sha256=$(openssl dgst -sha256 -hmac local-webhook-secret -hex < /tmp/delivery.json | awk '{print $NF}')"
    curl -sS -X POST http://coalesce-receiver.coalesce.svc.cluster.local/webhooks/github \
        -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
        -H 'X-GitHub-Event: pull_request' \
        -H "X-GitHub-Delivery: $(uuidgen | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')" \
        -H "X-Hub-Signature-256: $signature" \
        --data-binary @/tmp/delivery.json

The response names the Job it created — hello, the pull request number, and
a base36 epoch — hello-42-tjcy96 the first time this document ran. The name
is minted per delivery, so read the logs by the dispatch label rather than a
name this page cannot know:

    kubectl --context orbstack -n coalesce logs -l app.kubernetes.io/name=coalesce-dispatch --tail=5

prints hello, world. The webhook payload, event, and delivery ride into the
pod as annotations the receiver stamps, reachable by fieldRef — the hello
template shows the shape.

---

Log Tailing

The UI opens a dedicated WebSocket to tail pod logs through the server:

    /tail/{namespace}/{slug}/{job}?container={container}

The server finds the pod by label selector, waits for it to leave Pending,
and sends log_line events directly to that socket. Run state notifications
use the separate /events/{namespace}/{slug} WebSocket. Vite and the studio
container proxy both paths; the server image handles them directly.

---

Log Storage

Stow abstracts log storage. The local manifests point it at the coalesce-logs
PVC via STOW_KIND=local and STOW_PATH — do not remove those lines: without
them the code quietly defaults to /tmp inside the pod, every rebuild discards
the logs, and the first log click after a rebuild returns a storage error
that looks like a UI bug and is not one. Production sets STOW_KIND=google and
STOW_BUCKET instead.

---

Resetting

Dev data is disposable. The reset is the namespace, and only the namespace:

    kubectl --context orbstack delete namespace coalesce
    kubectl --context orbstack apply -f manifests/local/

Then check the reclaim chain held — PV cleanup is the local-path provisioner
scheduling a helper pod, not the API server deleting data, and if the
provisioner hiccups a Released PV lingers with its data intact, cluster-scoped
and invisible to the namespace reset:

    kubectl --context orbstack get pv

Nothing in Released means the chain held.

---

Layout

    bin/coalesce                    Executor entry point (zshctl shebang)
    share/coalesce/commands/        Executor commands (run/command.zsh is the event loop)
    cmd/web/main.go                 Go server
    cmd/web/migrations/             Database schema
    cmd/receiver/                   Webhook receiver
    cmd/operator/                   Readable source to ConfigMap controller
    run.html                        Old UI prototype (served at /run.html)
    test/                           Pipeline test definitions
    studio/two/                     Product UI (Vite, React, TypeScript)
    studio/                         UI package, shared transport, and exhibits
    manifests/local/                The local environment (this file's subject)
    manifests/                      CRD, portable operator, and older manifests
    Dockerfile                      Server image
    Dockerfile.receiver             Receiver image
    Dockerfile.operator             Static source operator image
    Dockerfile.dispatch             Local dispatch image (zsh, tini, zshctl)

---

Troubleshooting

Server pod crash-looping: check PostgreSQL first —

    kubectl --context orbstack get pods -n coalesce
    kubectl --context orbstack logs -n coalesce deployment/coalesce-web

Image not found (ErrImageNeverPull): the local build is missing —

    docker images coalesce-web

Run recorded under the wrong namespace: see Running a Pipeline; an override
was missed. The row lands where the executor's curls said, not where the Jobs
ran.

kubectl pointed somewhere unexpected:

    kubectl config current-context
